{:event/id "31356763-ac47-45eb-84ca-4d0b7024244f" :event/schema {:schema/root "8eb19ab0e4b70e205a84a627fd3e15c3ba79c0fd8c792d37e72f3c79511e0bf9" :schema/id :archaeology/finding-recorded :schema/hash "f2ad682fa2ac851a5d18b436849d3c59f07eb5f9ebe6179d231d7254b8d89848"} :event/type :archaeology/finding-recorded :event/stream "archaeology:findings:777b4ce0-c8f6-4e70-943f-27935c7ae3ec" :event/seq 1 :event/causes ["777b4ce0-c8f6-4e70-943f-27935c7ae3ec"] :event/actor "agent:chatgpt" :event/subject "archaeology:777b4ce0-c8f6-4e70-943f-27935c7ae3ec" :event/at "2026-09-02T13:08:15Z" :event/data {:finding/id :knoxx/openplanner-stable-graph-id-splits-occurrence-from-materialization :finding/shapes #{:graph :ledger :projection :identity} :finding/current-name "stable graph event ids + OpenPlanner EventAdmission + keyed graph materializers" :finding/candidate-name "OccurrenceLedger + KeyedMaterializedView<Key>" :finding/claim "Knoxx emits repeatable graph event ids from semantic node and edge addresses. OpenPlanner EventAdmission currently inserts every accepted envelope as a new events document, and the SDK-created events indexes do not establish uniqueness for the envelope id field. Separately, graph edges are upserted by source-node, target-node, and edge-kind, while node embeddings are upserted by node identity plus embedding coordinates. The current downstream law therefore separates admitted observations from keyed current-state materialization: a semantic graph id is not proven to be immutable occurrence identity at admission, even though it anchors convergent materialized graph views."}}
{:event/id "1b6c3910-2c4b-4aac-814a-9e07f9b93bd9" :event/schema {:schema/root "8eb19ab0e4b70e205a84a627fd3e15c3ba79c0fd8c792d37e72f3c79511e0bf9" :schema/id :archaeology/finding-recorded :schema/hash "f2ad682fa2ac851a5d18b436849d3c59f07eb5f9ebe6179d231d7254b8d89848"} :event/type :archaeology/finding-recorded :event/stream "archaeology:findings:777b4ce0-c8f6-4e70-943f-27935c7ae3ec" :event/seq 2 :event/causes ["31356763-ac47-45eb-84ca-4d0b7024244f"] :event/actor "agent:chatgpt" :event/subject "archaeology:777b4ce0-c8f6-4e70-943f-27935c7ae3ec" :event/at "2026-09-02T13:08:15Z" :event/data {:finding/id :knoxx/progress-event-bus-is-ephemeral-fanout-not-history :finding/shapes #{:queue :projection :runtime :history} :finding/current-name "kms-ingestion.api.event-bus/subscribers" :finding/candidate-name "EphemeralFanoutMailbox<Scope,Event>" :finding/claim "Each ingestion SSE subscriber receives a fresh process-local LinkedBlockingQueue registered under one job id; broadcast copies future events into every currently registered mailbox, poll preserves per-mailbox order, and unsubscribe deletes the mailbox and eventually the job entry. There is no replay or persistence authority, so this is neither History nor BoundedLog. The implementation also constructs the queue without an explicit capacity, so the source comment about dropping on a full queue does not establish the bounded-overflow law of BoundedWorkQueue."}}
{:event/id "fd246107-35b3-418c-8d4c-df0f4511743c" :event/schema {:schema/root "8eb19ab0e4b70e205a84a627fd3e15c3ba79c0fd8c792d37e72f3c79511e0bf9" :schema/id :archaeology/finding-recorded :schema/hash "f2ad682fa2ac851a5d18b436849d3c59f07eb5f9ebe6179d231d7254b8d89848"} :event/type :archaeology/finding-recorded :event/stream "archaeology:findings:777b4ce0-c8f6-4e70-943f-27935c7ae3ec" :event/seq 3 :event/causes ["1b6c3910-2c4b-4aac-814a-9e07f9b93bd9"] :event/actor "agent:chatgpt" :event/subject "archaeology:777b4ce0-c8f6-4e70-943f-27935c7ae3ec" :event/at "2026-09-02T13:08:15Z" :event/data {:finding/id :knoxx/sse-heartbeat-is-transport-keepalive-not-owner-liveness :finding/shapes #{:liveness :protocol :ownership :infra} :finding/current-name "SSE comment heartbeat + system-instance ownership" :finding/candidate-name "TransportKeepalive<Connection> != OwnershipLease<Owner>" :finding/claim "The ingestion SSE route sends a comment heartbeat when its subscriber mailbox poll is idle, keeping one client connection alive and checking terminal job state. Knoxx system-instance ownership separately remains plain process-id equality under an explicit single-writer assumption and says multi-instance ownership would require a liveness or heartbeat dimension. The existing SSE heartbeat supplies no ownership lease evidence; heartbeat is therefore an overloaded transport term here, and IncarnationFence remains intentionally weaker than a multi-writer lease."}}
